    {
      "part": "II",
      "kind": "mathematical-proof-clarification",
      "old": "The Laplace transform of \\(\\e^{tA_{QQ}}\\) is \\((zI-A_{QQ})^{-1}\\). Multiplication by the two coupling blocks gives the first identity. The remaining estimates follow directly from the assumed semigroup bound.",
      "new": "If \\(X_Q=\\{0\\}\\), the memory kernel and self-energy are both zero, so the identity is immediate. Otherwise write \\(B=A_{QQ}\\), which is a bounded operator on the hidden Banach space, and put \\(s(B)=\\max\\operatorname{Re}\\Spec(B)\\). Choose a real \\(\\beta\\) with \\(s(B)<\\beta<\\operatorname{Re}z\\). Spectral mapping and the spectral-radius formula give \\(r(\\e^B)=\\e^{s(B)}<\\e^\\beta\\), hence \\(\\norm{\\e^{nB}}\\leq M_\\beta\\e^{\\beta n}\\) for every integer \\(n\\geq0\\), after increasing \\(M_\\beta\\) to cover the finitely many initial values. Write \\(t=n+r\\), where \\(0\\leq r<1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\norm{\\e^{tB}}\\leq M_\\beta\\exp(\\norm B+\\max(0,-\\beta))\\e^{\\beta t}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\e^{-zt}\\e^{tB}\\) is Bochner integrable and tends to zero. Integrating its derivative gives both inverse identities for \\(R=\\int_0^\\infty\\e^{-zt}\\e^{tB}\\dd t\\), so \\(R=(zI-B)^{-1}\\). This is the bounded-generator growth principle in \\cite[Chapter IV, Corollary 3.12]{EngelNagel2000}. Multiplication by the coupling blocks gives the first identity. The remaining estimates follow directly from the assumed positive decay rate.",
      "reason": "Supply bounded Banach spectral-half-plane proof; preserve original broad scope."
    },
